## Performance Analysis — `CaseTransformExtensions.cs`

**Hot path identified:** `Underscore`, `Pascalize`, `Camelize` (millions of calls)

---

### Scan Execution Checklist

| Recipe | Hits |
|--------|------|
| `.ToLower()`/`.ToUpper()` without culture | 2 (L79, L121) |
| Chained Regex.Replace | 1 (L117-121) |
| `RegexOptions.Compiled` | 4 (L36-38, L215) |
| `[GeneratedRegex]` | 3 (L13-19) |
| `.Substring(` | 2 (L79) |
| `new Regex(` | 4 (L36-38, L215) |
| Unsealed classes | 1 (`InflectionRule`) |
| Sealed classes | 0 |

---

### Findings

#### 1. Triple Regex Chain + `.ToLower()` in `Underscore` (1 instance) 🔴 Critical
**Impact:** 4 intermediate string allocations per call × millions of calls = massive GC pressure  
**File:** CaseTransformExtensions.cs:L115-121  
**Fix:** Replace with a single-pass `StringBuilder` or `string.Create` approach that inserts underscores and lowercases in one traversal. Also use `ToLowerInvariant()` to avoid culture bugs.

```csharp
public static string Underscore(this string input)
{
    if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(input)) return input;

    var sb = new StringBuilder(input.Length + 4);
    for (int i = 0; i < input.Length; i++)
    {
        char c = input[i];
        if (c == '-' || c == ' ')
        {
            sb.Append('_');
        }
        else if (char.IsUpper(c))
        {
            if (i > 0 && !char.IsUpper(input[i - 1]))
                sb.Append('_');
            else if (i > 0 && char.IsUpper(input[i - 1]) && i + 1 < input.Length && char.IsLower(input[i + 1]))
                sb.Append('_');
            sb.Append(char.ToLowerInvariant(c));
        }
        else
        {
            sb.Append(c);
        }
    }
    return sb.ToString();
}
```

#### 2. Per-Match Allocations in `Pascalize` (1 instance) 🔴 Critical
**Impact:** Each regex match allocates: `match.Value` + `Substring(0,1)` + `.ToUpper()` + `Substring(1)` + concatenation = 5 allocations per word part × millions of calls  
**File:** CaseTransformExtensions.cs:L78-79  
**Fix:** Use `EnumerateMatches` with `StringBuilder` to avoid per-match string allocations:

```csharp
public static string Pascalize(this string input)
{
    if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(input)) return input;

    var sb = new StringBuilder(input.Length);
    foreach (ValueMatch vm in WordPartsRegex().EnumerateMatches(input))
    {
        var span = input.AsSpan(vm.Index, vm.Length);
        sb.Append(char.ToUpperInvariant(span[0]));
        sb.Append(span[1..]);
    }
    return sb.ToString();
}
```

#### 3. Extra Allocation in `Camelize` (1 instance) 🟡 Moderate
**Impact:** `pascalized[1..]` allocates a new string (entire result minus first char) on every call  
**File:** CaseTransformExtensions.cs:L101  
**Fix:** Combine with `Pascalize` logic — write the first char lowercase directly, avoiding the second string allocation entirely. Or use `string.Create`:

```csharp
public static string Camelize(this string input)
{
    var pascalized = input.Pascalize();
    if (pascalized.Length == 0) return pascalized;
    return string.Create(pascalized.Length, pascalized, (span, s) =>
    {
        s.AsSpan().CopyTo(span);
        span[0] = char.ToLowerInvariant(span[0]);
    });
}
